  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nStarting midweek we'll begin to see a pattern change as the\naforementioned upper Low progresses eastward, with the\nassociated upper shortwave trough flattening out the ridge by\nWednesday. At the surface, a cold front is progged to push\nsoutheast through the area beginning late Wednesday afternoon \ninto Thursday. With a moist airmass out ahead of the front \n(dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s) and moderate instability\n(MLCAPE upwards of 1500-3000 J/kg), showers and thunderstorms \nare likely (60-90+%) to develop along the front and push \nsoutheast Wednesday evening into Thursday. Right now, models \nshow a linear storm mode, as bulk shear vectors (20-30kts) look \nto be parallel to the front. This would lead to a Marginal risk \nfor damaging winds if any of these storms can become more \norganized, though this remains lower confidence as there's the \npotential for convection to be undercut by the front (as \nmentioned in SPCs discussion). \n\nBefore the frontal passage makes its way through the area late\nWednesday, the heat is expected to continue, with highs once\nagain reaching the mid 90s to low 100s and heat index values up\nto 100-110. Due to some remaining uncertainty regarding the\ntiming of the front, decided to hold off extending the Heat\nheadlines into Wednesday, however if trends continue as-is,\nwe'll likely need the heat headlines extended one more day.",
